sections.content:
TypeScript can infer the types of properties based on their values.

Example

const car = {

  type: "Toyota",

};

car.type = "Ford"; // no error

car.type = 2; // Error: Type 'number' is not assignable to type 'string'.

Try it Yourself »

Optional properties are properties that don't have to be defined in the object definition.

Example without an optional property

const car: { type: string, mileage: number } = { // Error: Property 'mileage' is missing in type '{ type: string;
}' but required in type '{ type: string; mileage: number; }'.

  type: "Toyota",

};

car.mileage = 2000;

Example with an optional property

const car: { type: string, mileage?: number } = { // no error

  type: "Toyota"

};

car.mileage = 2000;

Try it Yourself »

code_examples.code:
enum CardinalDirections {

  North,

  East,

  South,

  West

}

let currentDirection = CardinalDirections.North;

// logs 0

console.log(currentDirection);

// throws error as 'North' is not a valid enum

currentDirection = 'North'; // Error: "North" is not assignable to type 'CardinalDirections'.

sections.content:
By default, enums will initialize the first value to 0 and add 1 to each additional value:

Example

enum CardinalDirections {

  North,

  East,

  South,

  West

}

let currentDirection = CardinalDirections.North;

// logs 0

console.log(currentDirection);

// throws error as 'North' is not a valid enum

currentDirection = 'North'; // Error: "North" is not assignable to type 'CardinalDirections'.

Try it Yourself »

You can set the value of the first numeric enum and have it auto increment from that:

Example

enum CardinalDirections {

  North = 1,

  East,

  South,

  West

}

// logs 1

console.log(CardinalDirections.North);

// logs 4

console.log(CardinalDirections.West);

Try it Yourself »

You can assign unique number values for each enum value.

Then the values will not be incremented automatically:

Example

enum StatusCodes {

  NotFound = 404,

  Success = 200,

  Accepted = 202,

  BadRequest = 400

}

// logs 404

console.log(StatusCodes.NotFound);

// logs 200

console.log(StatusCodes.Success);

Try it Yourself »

Enums can also contain strings.

This is more common than numeric enums, because of their readability and intent.

Example

enum CardinalDirections {

  North = 'North',

  East = "East",

  South = "South",

  West = "West"

};

// logs "North"

console.log(CardinalDirections.North);

// logs "West"

console.log(CardinalDirections.West);

Try it Yourself »

Technically, you can mix and match string and numeric enum values, but it is recommended not to do so.

